The port rewrites upstream's Python in Elixir to run in a plugin on HAL-C2's MC. Each
file in mc/ names the upstream files it was ported from and what it changed. In short:

- The loop guard hears each turn's tool calls from the MC as they end, instead of from
  an agent CLI's hooks, and keeps its state in the plugin's process, not in files
  between hook processes.
- The judge is the user's text generation model in HAL-C2, run in the background as
  a job of the plugin's; the in-line (sampled) judge is not ported. The judge's verdict
  carries the redirect, which upstream wrote with a separate call.
- A redirect is delivered as a steer of the turn under way, with a notice in the
  thread that says why. The plugin never stops a turn itself: the last step is a
  notice that offers the person a stop.
- What counts as stuck is learned per project and provider from turns that ended
  green, kept as JSON in the plugin's data directory.
- Tool pruning keeps upstream's evidence rule and protected sets, Codex's
  capability packages (two demotions per session at most, three times the
  evidence for image/media) and its modes (auto after a quiet spell, propose,
  off), with restore that sticks. It differs in how it is fed and applied:
  the evidence comes from turns the MC hears (and earlier turns read back from
  a thread, folded as imported, which on its own never applies under auto),
  and a decision takes effect through the MC as a provider conversation
  starts, so no provider settings file is read or written and there is no
  catalog probe -- the tools taken as loaded are the ones the MC can turn off
  (upstream's live Codex packages exactly; for Claude, its known built-in tools
  outside the protected sets, so other tools, MCP ones included, are never
  proposed until that list grows). The experiments' per-task tool judge is not
  ported: the plugin never calls a model to prune, as upstream's plugin does not.
- Upstream's micro-skills and distiller are not part of this port yet.
